  "year": "1981",
  "subtitle": "The world's first notebook computer — a complete self-contained mobile office with a built-in microprinter that collapsed field data and hard-copy output into one continuous workflow",
  "why_scout_cares": "The HX-20 is the museum's complete mobile-computing output artifact: the first notebook-sized portable that integrated a functional dot-matrix microprinter into the chassis, turning data entry and hard-copy output from separate worksteps into a single continuous interaction. A field engineer or route accountant could enter data on the full-transit keyboard, run a BASIC calculation, and immediately hand a printed receipt to a customer — all while standing in the field. The printer company made the first printer-embedded computer. Distinct from all other portable/mobile exhibits (Poqet PC, Z88, Psion, Typecorder): none of them has an integral printer, and the printer is the whole point.",
  "search_terms": ["Epson HX-20 notebook computer built-in microprinter 1981", "first notebook computer Epson HX-20 printer"],
  "research": {
    "title": "Epson HX-20",
